"""Focused editorial additions for cycle 7, queue positions 151–200.

Each page receives one concrete clarification at its weakest transition.  The
renderer preserves the original prompts, curator exchanges, and other dialogue.
"""

EDITS = {
    "ethics/what-are-ethics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="A moral foundation must explain authority, not merely repeat preference", paragraph="Saying that an action is disliked, illegal, or socially costly does not yet explain why anyone morally ought to avoid it. A proposed foundation must connect facts about suffering, agency, fairness, flourishing, divine command, or rational consistency to a reason that can survive disagreement. The central test is therefore comparative: which bridge premise is being used, who could reasonably reject it, and what would follow if it failed?"),
    "ethics/is-vs-ought/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="A bridge principle moves the dispute; it does not make the dispute disappear", paragraph="Suppose a policy predictably causes avoidable suffering. The descriptive finding matters, but the conclusion that the policy ought to change also needs a premise such as ‘avoidable suffering counts against a policy.’ That premise may be compelling, yet it remains normative. Naming it improves the argument because critics can now challenge the evidence, the bridge principle, or the proposed remedy instead of talking past one another."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/how-minds-are-changed/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Reactance is the impulse to defend a choice when freedom feels threatened", paragraph="A useful self-check is to notice when the manner of persuasion—not the evidence—creates the urge to resist. If a claim feels controlling, restate it as a question you are free to test: ‘What evidence would move me either way?’ This restores ownership without granting the original belief immunity. The goal is not to become pressure-proof, but to keep threatened autonomy from deciding the conclusion in advance."),
    "economics/economic-comparisons/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Income comparisons depend on the unit, year, and question being asked", paragraph="A global mean, a national mean, and a median household income answer different questions. Currency conversion also matters: market exchange rates help compare internationally traded goods, while purchasing-power adjustments better approximate local living standards. Any table should therefore state the reference year, whether values are per person or per household, whether they are means or medians, and whether the comparison concerns cash income or material living conditions."),
    "epistemology/preponderance-of-evidence/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="A decision threshold should not masquerade as a threshold of truth", paragraph="In a civil trial, ‘more likely than not’ can be a practical rule for reaching a verdict. It does not imply that belief itself becomes binary at 50.1 percent. Evidence may support one account somewhat more than another while leaving substantial uncertainty. Keeping confidence separate from the rule for acting prevents a procedural cutoff from being mistaken for a sudden discovery of certainty."),
    "philosophy-of-language/what-is-language/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Language extends thought by making distinctions shareable and revisable", paragraph="Words do more than label ideas already complete in the mind. A vocabulary can make a previously blurry contrast easier to notice, preserve it across time, and expose it to correction by other people. Yet naming can also create false solidity: once a category has a crisp label, we may assume nature contains an equally crisp boundary. Language is therefore both a cognitive tool and a recurring source of conceptual overconfidence."),
    "miscellany/domains-of-aesthetics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Aesthetic attention should be integrated by value, not assigned a universal quota", paragraph="There is no defensible percentage of life that every person should devote to art. The practical question is whether aesthetic experience is doing valuable work—deepening attention, enlarging emotional range, restoring energy, sustaining community, or cultivating craft—without crowding out urgent obligations. A ten-minute daily practice may transform one life; another person may reasonably organize a career around the same goods."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/conspiracies-misunderstanding-human-nature/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Supernatural belief and conspiracy belief share pressures, not a single cause", paragraph="Both can recruit agency detection, pattern hunger, identity protection, and a preference for intentional stories over impersonal complexity. But resemblance is not identity: a supernatural belief may be culturally inherited without involving a coordinated cover-up, while a conspiracy claim may concern entirely ordinary actors. The useful comparison identifies specific reasoning habits without treating every religious or political belief as one psychological species."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/functionalism-subjectivity/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Functional explanation addresses organization, while the felt quality remains the pressure point", paragraph="A functionalist can explain pain by the role it plays: tissue damage produces aversive processing, attention, learning, avoidance, and reports. The critic then asks why that organization is accompanied by a felt hurt rather than occurring ‘in the dark.’ Functionalism gains clarity when it admits this explanatory gap directly and argues that a complete account of the relevant organization may still be the best available route to explaining subjectivity."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/subjective-objective-free-will/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Agency language remains useful when its level of description is explicit", paragraph="A determinist can say that a person chose tea because the decision flowed through that person’s reasons, memories, and preferences, while denying that the choice escaped every prior cause. The apparent contradiction dissolves if ‘free’ means responsive self-direction at the psychological level rather than exemption from causation at the physical level. Disagreement then shifts to whether that thinner freedom is sufficient for responsibility."),
    "economics/wealth-creation/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Wealth grows when coordination produces more usable value from limited inputs", paragraph="Imagine two workshops with the same labor and raw material. One loses time to poor tools, uncertain contracts, and repeated mistakes; the other improves its process, shares reliable knowledge, and produces more durable goods. The extra wealth is not merely a larger pile of money. It is the increase in useful output, capability, and future options—though who owns those gains remains a separate distributive question."),
    "epistemology/case-1-credence-complexity/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Credence depends jointly on source reliability and the prior plausibility of the claim", paragraph="A usually reliable neighbor saying that a package arrived deserves high confidence; the same neighbor reporting a dragon in the driveway does not. Conversely, a notorious gossip can still report an ordinary rainstorm correctly. A disciplined estimate therefore combines what is known about the speaker with what is known about the kind of event, while leaving room for new evidence to revise both judgments."),
    "epistemology/case-4-recursive-credences/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Circular trust stabilizes only when the network has evidence from outside itself", paragraph="If Ana trusts Bo because Cara endorses him, Cara trusts Dev, and Dev ultimately trusts Ana, repetition alone has created no independent support. The loop becomes informative only when some members also have direct observations, reliable track records, or incentives that make coordinated error less likely. Recursive confidence can converge mathematically while remaining epistemically hollow; stability is not the same as contact with reality."),
    "humanistic-philosophies/accounting-for-x/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="The debate turns on whether explanation is being confused with ownership", paragraph="A Christian may offer a metaphysical story about why logic exists, while a non-Christian may treat logical laws as necessary relations, features of valid inference, or abstractions from consistent reasoning. Neither view gains priority merely by claiming to ‘account for’ logic. The productive question is what explanatory burden exists, what each proposal adds, and whether the proposal predicts or clarifies anything that its rivals cannot."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/dangers-carrot-stick/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Inoculation begins by separating the claim from the reward or threat attached to it", paragraph="When a message promises paradise for agreement or catastrophe for doubt, first imagine hearing the same incentive attached to a rival claim. Then ask what evidence would remain if the emotional payoff were removed. This symmetry test does not prove the message false; it prevents desire and fear from being counted twice—once as motivation and again as supposed evidence."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/dangers-the-notion-of-fate/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Fate-talk can describe acceptance, but it becomes dangerous when it replaces causal inquiry", paragraph="After an irreversible event, saying ‘it was meant to be’ may provide comfort. Before or during a decision, the same phrase can hide preventable causes, excuse passivity, or hand authority to someone claiming special knowledge of destiny. A safer formulation is: the past is fixed, the future remains uncertain, and present choices can still alter which possibilities become real."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/testing-ideologies/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Unfulfilled promises justify suspicion when the promise and test were specified in advance", paragraph="Failure is informative only when the ideology clearly stated what should happen, under what conditions, and within what period. Otherwise defenders can continually redefine success, blame invisible interference, or postpone the deadline. Suspicion should rise most sharply when a system claims credit for every favorable outcome but treats every failure as irrelevant to the system itself."),
    "rational-thought/scope-of-influence/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Large ambitions deserve scrutiny when they substitute scale for neglected local agency", paragraph="Wanting to influence millions can be admirable, but it may also protect the ego from smaller forms of accountability. It is easier to imagine transforming civilization than to repair one damaged relationship, teach one person carefully, or build one dependable institution. The test is not ‘think small’; it is whether the grand aim grows from practiced competence and real service rather than compensating for their absence."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/dangers-ideologies-of-mystery/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Beauty and mystery can invite inquiry without licensing belief", paragraph="A mysterious doctrine may evoke awe, but that reaction is compatible with truth, falsehood, metaphor, and deliberate obscurity. A calm response is: ‘The mystery gives me a reason to explore; what gives me a reason to accept this particular explanation?’ This preserves wonder while requiring the ideology to earn the inferential step from emotional resonance to factual commitment."),
    "economics/economic-stability/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Some unpredictability creates opportunity; systemic unpredictability destroys the conditions for using it", paragraph="An entrepreneur may profit from uncertain tastes, changing technology, or a temporary price mismatch. That is different from uncertainty about whether contracts will be enforced, currency will retain basic usefulness, or political violence will interrupt exchange. Productive risk occurs inside a sufficiently stable framework; when the framework itself becomes unreliable, arbitrage opportunities can coexist with broad economic decay."),
    "economics/minimum-wage/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Minimum-wage arguments should state whose necessities and which policy goal matter", paragraph="The claim that a wage must cover ‘necessities’ is incomplete until household size, local costs, hours, benefits, and the intended unit of support are named. A wage floor might aim to prevent coercively low pay, reduce in-work poverty, strengthen bargaining power, or shift costs away from public assistance. Those goals overlap, but they are not identical and can justify different thresholds."),
    "economics/schools-of-economic-thought/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="History tests policy bundles under particular institutions, never a school in laboratory isolation", paragraph="A recession under a nominally Keynesian government does not by itself refute Keynesian economics, just as growth under tax cuts does not isolate one supply-side mechanism. Countries mix monetary rules, welfare systems, trade exposure, demographics, legal institutions, and inherited shocks. Historical comparison is still useful when claims are narrowed to mechanisms and rival explanations are made explicit."),
    "economics/taxation/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="A tax sweet spot is plural, local, and discovered through behavioral response", paragraph="The revenue-maximizing rate is not automatically the welfare-maximizing rate, and neither is uniform across income, consumption, land, inheritance, or pollution. Policymakers must watch avoidance, labor and investment responses, administrative cost, distribution, public benefits, and legitimacy together. The useful image is not one peak but several interacting curves whose shapes change with institutions and time."),
    "economics/what-are-moral-hazards/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Moral hazard now names risk-shifting under protection, not moral failure in general", paragraph="The core structure is that protection from consequences changes behavior or weakens incentives to prevent loss. Insurance is the classic setting, but guarantees, bailouts, limited liability, and delegated decisions can create similar pressures. The term becomes misleading when applied to any bad incentive whatsoever: a moral hazard requires some separation between who chooses the risk and who bears its cost."),
    "economics/what-is-economics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Economic systems should be judged by several outcomes and by how they adapt", paragraph="Output and income matter, but so do stability, mobility, health, leisure, ecological cost, distribution, freedom, and resilience to shocks. No single score settles the comparison because societies reasonably weight these goods differently. A stronger assessment states the weights, checks whether gains in one dimension conceal losses in another, and asks whether the system can correct predictable failures without losing its productive strengths."),
    "epistemology/black-boxes-epistemology/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Repeated success earns calibrated reliance, not unlimited trust", paragraph="A diagnostic model that performs well across hospitals and patient groups may deserve use even before every internal feature is interpretable. But reliance should remain bounded by the tested population, the cost of error, monitoring for drift, and comparison with alternatives. Predictive success answers ‘does it work here?’ more directly than ‘why does it work?’; high-stakes use often needs responsible answers to both."),
    "epistemology/core-deep-rationality/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Four characters show why sound method and deep self-scrutiny can come apart", paragraph="A careful statistician may reason impeccably about a study yet never question the career incentive shaping which studies get chosen: strong core rationality, weaker deep rationality. A reflective activist may recognize identity-protective motives yet use poor causal evidence: stronger depth, weaker core. The best judgment joins competent inference with examination of the goals, frames, and incentives that selected the problem."),
    "epistemology/deduction-utility-and-issues/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Instantiating a valid form reveals whether the premises fit the real case", paragraph="The form ‘All A are B; x is A; therefore x is B’ guarantees the conclusion only if its terms are used consistently and both premises are true. Replacing the letters with ‘All licensed surgeons completed training; Lee is a licensed surgeon; therefore Lee completed training’ makes hidden questions visible: which jurisdiction, what counts as training, and is the licensing record accurate? Formal validity organizes scrutiny; it does not replace it."),
    "epistemology/extraordinary-claims/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="The Sagan standard is a reminder about likelihood, not a license for arbitrary dismissal", paragraph="Evidence should be proportioned to how surprising a claim is relative to well-supported background knowledge. That does not mean an authority may label an unwelcome proposal ‘extraordinary’ and refuse to name what would count. A fair use specifies the relevant prior expectations, the reliability of the evidence, plausible error routes, and the observations that would genuinely shift confidence."),
    "epistemology/induction-utility-and-issues/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Induction is strongest when the sampling process and possible changes are visible", paragraph="Drawing many red cards from a well-shuffled deck supports a prediction about the remaining cards only because the deck’s composition and sampling procedure constrain the possibilities. Predictions about economies, species, or technologies are harder: the process can change while we observe it. Inductive confidence should therefore track sample quality, mechanism, stability, and the distance between observed cases and the case being predicted."),
    "epistemology/pragmatic-considerations-vs-epistemic-assessments/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Practical urgency can lower the threshold for action without raising the probability of truth", paragraph="If a smoke alarm sounds, leaving the building may be rational even when fire is unlikely because the cost of delay is severe. The alarm’s probability does not become larger merely because evacuation is prudent. This distinction allows urgent, compassionate, or precautionary action without redescribing weak evidence as strong evidence—a crucial defense against motivated belief."),
    "epistemology/predictive-power/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Prediction is central, but explanation, calibration, and transfer also matter", paragraph="A model can predict well by exploiting a temporary correlation, remain badly calibrated about its own confidence, or fail when moved to a new population. Rational assessment should therefore ask whether predictions are accurate, probabilities match observed frequencies, the method survives changed conditions, and the explanation supports intervention. Predictive power is a demanding test, but not the only dimension of dependable reasoning."),
    "epistemology/presuppositions/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Axioms stop a formal regress, but their use is still answerable to experience", paragraph="A formal system can stipulate axioms and derive consequences without proving those axioms inside the same system. Choosing that system for reasoning about the world is a further act. We compare its coherence, usefulness, fit with repeated experience, and success relative to alternatives. This does not make every logical rule a simple empirical generalization; it shows why confidence in applying a framework is not insulated from practice."),
    "epistemology/properly-basic-beliefs/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="An experience of God may be direct as experience without making its interpretation incorrigible", paragraph="A person can be immediately aware of awe, presence, dependence, or a seeming encounter. What is directly accessible is the experience; identifying its cause as a particular deity adds interpretation. The comparison with ‘I feel happy’ therefore reaches only partway. Both can be sincere first-person reports, while the external-object claim carries additional possibilities of error and requires additional support."),
    "epistemology/reasoned-probabilities-and-decisions/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="A leap of faith is a decision beyond the evidence, not evidence added by decision", paragraph="Someone may commit to a relationship, vocation, or rescue attempt before success is likely because waiting has costs and action can help create the outcome. That can be practically courageous without changing the present evidence. Calling the commitment a leap of faith is clearest when it names accepted risk and chosen value, rather than an unexplained increase in confidence that the desired proposition is true."),
    "epistemology/the-abuse-of-self-evident/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="‘Self-evident’ has philosophical weight only when it identifies a disciplined stopping point", paragraph="Some propositions appear undeniable once their terms are understood; others merely feel obvious because they are familiar, emotionally charged, or socially protected. The label cannot do the argumentative work by itself. A responsible appeal explains whether the claim is definitional, logically unavoidable, directly experiential, or a foundational commitment—and then tests whether reasonable disagreement exposes hidden premises."),
    "epistemology/what-is-doubt/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Rational doubt is confidence withheld for reasons that track the claim", paragraph="Doubt is not automatically virtuous. It becomes rational when it responds to missing evidence, unreliable sources, live alternatives, internal tension, or a poor record of prediction. It becomes evasive when standards rise only for disliked conclusions or when every possible observation is dismissed. The discipline is symmetry: comparable claims should face comparable burdens, adjusted for stakes and prior plausibility."),
    "epistemology/what-is-evidence/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Absence becomes evidence when the missing observation was genuinely expected", paragraph="Finding no elephant in a small room strongly supports its absence because an elephant would be hard to miss. Finding no rare bird during a five-minute forest walk provides much weaker support because detection was unlikely even if the bird was present. The evidential force of absence depends on search quality, detection probability, and whether the hypothesis predicted an observable trace."),
    "ethics/assisted-suicide/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Scoring ethical theories can expose tradeoffs, but the numbers are not moral measurements", paragraph="A table may compare how well theories address autonomy, suffering, consent, abuse risk, equality, and professional duty. Assigning scores can make hidden priorities visible, yet the totals depend on chosen criteria and weights. A one-point change can create a spurious winner. The table is best used to locate disagreement and neglected harms, not to convert a tragic decision into arithmetic certainty."),
    "ethics/conditions-for-culpability/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Impaired control can reduce culpability without erasing every responsibility", paragraph="Compulsion, intoxication, coercion, ignorance, and mental disorder affect different conditions: awareness, voluntariness, foresight, and ability to do otherwise. They should not be treated as one all-or-nothing excuse. A person may be less blameworthy for an immediate act yet still responsible for an earlier avoidable choice that created the danger. Moral assessment must track the relevant time and capacity."),
    "ethics/evidences-of-moral-facts/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Shared moral reactions support psychological facts more directly than mind-independent moral facts", paragraph="Widespread revulsion at cruelty is important evidence about human dispositions, social coordination, and the kinds of lives creatures like us can bear. A realist may argue that convergence is best explained by sensitivity to moral truth; a non-realist may explain it through shared vulnerability, evolution, learning, and institutions. The reaction is data for both accounts, so the philosophical work lies in comparing their additional commitments and explanatory reach."),
    "ethics/model-of-ethical-dynamics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Affordability constrains ethical behavior without determining it", paragraph="People with time, security, information, and institutional support often have more room to act generously or absorb the cost of principle. Scarcity can narrow that room, but it does not mechanically produce vice, nor does privilege guarantee virtue. In a model, affordability should be one influence among norms, incentives, empathy, enforcement, identity, and feedback—not a moral verdict attached to income."),
    "ethics/self-evident-morality/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="The force of a moral intuition and the objectivity of its content are separate questions", paragraph="An intuition can be immediate, stable, and psychologically difficult to reject while still reflecting upbringing, shared biology, or framing. Conversely, the possibility of influence does not automatically make the judgment false. The careful move is to treat intuition as evidence that begins inquiry, then test consistency, scope, counterexamples, rival explanations, and what happens when equally sincere intuitions conflict."),
    "humanistic-philosophies/personal-cosmic-meaning/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Personal meaning can be real in a life without being written into the cosmos", paragraph="Raising a child, mastering a craft, or sustaining a friendship can organize attention, sacrifice, memory, and identity in ways that are neither arbitrary nor universal. Their value depends on persons and relationships, but dependence does not make them illusory—music also depends on hearers. Cosmic meaning adds a different claim: that reality as a whole contains a purpose. The two questions should not borrow evidence from each other."),
    "humanistic-philosophies/russell-on-faith/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Choose terms that reveal whether confidence comes from evidence, trust, or commitment", paragraph="‘Faith’ can mean belief without sufficient evidence, trust grounded in a relationship, loyalty under uncertainty, or a religious orientation that combines all three. Arguments slide when one meaning supplies emotional dignity while another makes the epistemic claim. Replacing the word with the intended term—confidence, trust, hope, commitment, or unsupported belief—often reveals the real agreement or disagreement."),
    "humanistic-philosophies/the-legitimacy-of-divine-revelation/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Community can preserve testimony, but authority cannot independently confirm its own authority", paragraph="A community may transmit texts, practices, linguistic knowledge, and historical memory with impressive fidelity. Yet agreement inside that tradition does not by itself establish the divine source of what it preserves, especially when rival communities use parallel methods. Confirmation must add evidence not wholly dependent on the claim under review, or the authority and revelation merely support each other in a circle."),
    "humanistic-philosophies/unpalatable-religions/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="An unpleasant doctrine can be true, but unpleasantness is not evidence of truth", paragraph="Some religious leaders treat austerity, fear, or sacrifice as proof that a teaching has not been softened to attract followers. That inference fails: invented systems can also be harsh, and accurate descriptions can be comforting. Palatability and truth vary independently. The right assessment asks what evidence supports the doctrine and what human incentives might favor either an appealing or an intimidating version."),
    "introduction/careers-in-philosophy/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Philosophy improves career options when its skills are translated into visible work", paragraph="Employers rarely hire for ‘having studied difficult ideas’ in the abstract. They hire for writing a clear brief, finding a hidden assumption, comparing uncertain options, explaining a dispute fairly, or revising a decision when evidence changes. A philosophy student should therefore pair the degree with artifacts and experience—research memos, policy analysis, teaching, data work, mediation, or domain knowledge—that make those transferable habits observable."),
    "introduction/philosophy-higher-education/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="The best value depends on funding, intellectual fit, supervision, and likely next steps", paragraph="A famous department can be a poor bargain if debt is high, mentoring is scarce, or its strengths do not match the student’s questions. A less prominent program may offer close supervision, language training, interdisciplinary access, and full funding. Quality-to-cost comparisons should include net price, completion support, placement evidence, intellectual climate, and the value of the education even if an academic job never follows."),
    "introduction/womens-interest-in-philosophy/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Participation gaps require stage-by-stage evidence, not a story about intrinsic interest", paragraph="A gender gap among majors could emerge from early exposure, classroom climate, role models, grading, perceived career paths, harassment, stereotype threat, or attrition later in training. Aggregate enrollment cannot tell us which mechanism dominates. The responsible inquiry follows cohorts across stages, compares institutions, listens to reported experience, and avoids turning a historically variable outcome into a claim about fixed preference."),
}
